/** @mainpage Qore ml Module

    @tableofcontents

    @section mlintro ml Module Introduction

    The \c ml module provides machine learning algorithms for %Qore programs, enabling
    thread-safe ML inference and analysis for streaming data pipelines without external
    dependencies like Python.

    This module wraps the Eigen C++ linear algebra library to provide efficient, native
    implementations of common ML algorithms. Optional ONNX Runtime support allows running
    pre-trained models from frameworks like PyTorch, scikit-learn, and TensorFlow.

    @section mlalgorithms Available Algorithms

    @subsection mlalgo_table Algorithm Overview

    |Algorithm|Type|Use Case|
    |---|---|---|
    |@ref ML::IsolationForest "IsolationForest"|Anomaly Detection|Finds anomalies by measuring isolation in random trees|
    |@ref ML::LOF "LOF"|Anomaly Detection|Detects local density-based anomalies using k-nearest neighbors|
    |@ref ML::DBSCAN "DBSCAN"|Clustering|Discovers arbitrarily shaped clusters without specifying k|
    |@ref ML::KMeans "KMeans"|Clustering|Partitions data into k groups with optional online updates|
    |@ref ML::GMM "GMM"|Clustering|Probabilistic soft clustering using Gaussian mixtures (EM algorithm)|
    |@ref ML::LinearRegression "LinearRegression"|Regression|Predicts a target variable from numeric features (OLS)|
    |@ref ML::HoltWinters "HoltWinters"|Time Series|Triple exponential smoothing forecasting with seasonality|
    |@ref ML::SeasonalDecomposition "SeasonalDecomposition"|Time Series|Extracts trend, seasonal, and residual components|
    |@ref ML::PCA "PCA"|Dimensionality Reduction|Reduces dimensions while preserving variance|
    |@ref ML::OnnxModel "OnnxModel"|Inference|Runs pre-trained ONNX models (optional, requires ONNX Runtime)|

    @subsection mlalgo_anomaly Anomaly Detection

    - @ref ML::IsolationForest "IsolationForest" — Global anomaly detection using random partitioning.
      Anomalous points are isolated in fewer splits than normal points. Best for identifying global
      outliers in high-dimensional data.
    - @ref ML::LOF "LOF" — Local Outlier Factor detects anomalies based on local density deviation
      relative to neighbors. Catches anomalies that are normal in one region but unusual in another.

    @subsection mlalgo_clustering Clustering

    - @ref ML::DBSCAN "DBSCAN" — Density-based clustering that discovers clusters of arbitrary
      shape. Does not require specifying the number of clusters. Points not belonging to any
      cluster are labeled as noise.
    - @ref ML::KMeans "KMeans" — Classic k-means clustering with k-means++ initialization and
      optional online (mini-batch) updates for evolving data.
    - @ref ML::GMM "GMM" — Gaussian Mixture Model provides probabilistic (soft) cluster
      assignments. Each point gets a probability for each cluster, enabling uncertainty
      estimation and overlapping cluster detection.

    @subsection mlalgo_regression Regression

    - @ref ML::LinearRegression "LinearRegression" — Ordinary least squares linear regression.
      Fits a linear model y = Xw + b to predict a target variable from one or more numeric
      features. Reports R-squared, coefficients, and intercept.

    @subsection mlalgo_timeseries Time Series

    - @ref ML::HoltWinters "HoltWinters" — Triple exponential smoothing (Holt-Winters method)
      for forecasting values with trend and seasonal patterns. Supports additive and
      multiplicative seasonality with optional damped trends.
    - @ref ML::SeasonalDecomposition "SeasonalDecomposition" — Decomposes a time series into
      trend, seasonal, and residual components. Supports additive and multiplicative models.

    @subsection mlalgo_dimreduce Dimensionality Reduction

    - @ref ML::PCA "PCA" — Principal Component Analysis projects high-dimensional data onto
      principal components that capture the most variance. Supports fixed component count or
      automatic selection via variance threshold.

    @subsection mlalgo_onnx ONNX Model Inference

    When compiled with ONNX Runtime support, the module provides:
    - @ref ML::OnnxModel "OnnxModel" — Loads and runs pre-trained ONNX models for inference.
      Supports float, double, int32, and int64 tensor types. ONNX Runtime sessions are
      thread-safe for concurrent inference after model loading.

    To check ONNX availability at runtime:
    @code{.py}
hash<ML::MLCapabilities> caps = ML::ml_get_capabilities();
if (caps.has_onnx) {
    ML::OnnxModel model("/path/to/model.onnx");
    hash<auto> result = model.run({"input": data});
}
    @endcode

    This module is released under the MIT license (see \c COPYING.MIT in the source distribution for
    more information). The module is tagged as such in the module's header (meaning it can be loaded
    unconditionally regardless of how the %Qore library was initialized).

    To use the module in a %Qore script, use the \c %%requires directive as follows:
    @code %requires ml @endcode

    @section mlthread Thread Safety

    All classes in this module are designed for thread-safe usage:
    - Each class instance has independent state (no shared mutable globals)
    - After fitting, scoring/prediction methods can be called concurrently from
      multiple threads
    - Each instance uses its own random number generator
    - ONNX Runtime sessions are inherently thread-safe for inference

    @section mlexamples Usage Examples

    @subsection mlexample_iforest Anomaly Detection with IsolationForest

    @code{.py}
%requires ml

# Create and configure
ML::IsolationForest iforest({"n_trees": 100, "sample_size": 256, "threshold": 0.6});

# Fit on normal data
iforest.fit(training_records);

# Score new records
hash<ML::IsolationForestResult> result = iforest.score(new_record);
if (result.is_anomaly) {
    printf("Anomaly detected! Score: %f\n", result.anomaly_score);
}
    @endcode

    @subsection mlexample_linreg Linear Regression

    @code{.py}
%requires ml

# Create model
ML::LinearRegression lr({"fit_intercept": True});

# Fit on data with a target field
lr.fit(training_data, "price");

# Predict
hash<ML::LinearRegressionResult> result = lr.predict(new_record);
printf("Predicted price: %f (R²: %f)\n", result.prediction, result.r_squared);
    @endcode

    @subsection mlexample_gmm Probabilistic Clustering with GMM

    @code{.py}
%requires ml

ML::GMM gmm({"n_components": 3, "seed": 42});
gmm.fit(data);

hash<ML::GMMResult> result = gmm.predict(new_point);
printf("Cluster: %d (probability: %f)\n", result.cluster, result.max_probability);
printf("All probabilities: %y\n", result.probabilities);
    @endcode

    @subsection mlexample_lof Local Outlier Factor

    @code{.py}
%requires ml

ML::LOF lof({"k": 20, "threshold": 1.5});
lof.fit(reference_data);

hash<ML::LOFResult> result = lof.score(test_point);
if (result.is_anomaly) {
    printf("Local outlier detected! LOF score: %f\n", result.lof_score);
}
    @endcode

    @subsection mlexample_onnx ONNX Model Inference

    @code{.py}
%requires ml

hash<ML::MLCapabilities> caps = ML::ml_get_capabilities();
if (!caps.has_onnx) {
    printf("ONNX Runtime not available\n");
} else {
    ML::OnnxModel model("/path/to/model.onnx");

    # Check model metadata
    hash<ML::OnnxModelInfo> info = model.getModelInfo();
    printf("Model: %s (version %d)\n", info.producer_name, info.version);

    # Run inference
    hash<auto> result = model.run({"X": ((1.0, 2.0, 3.0),)});
    printf("Output: %y\n", result);
}
    @endcode

